>
  <h2>What is documented information, and how is it different from a record?</h2>
  <p>
    Documented information is ISO 9001:2015&rsquo;s single term for anything you write down and control so your quality
    management system (QMS) works. It covers two things people used to talk about separately: documents and records. A
    <strong>document</strong> tells people what to do, such as a procedure, a specification or a drawing. A <strong>record</strong> proves
    what happened, such as a completed inspection form, a signed-off approval or a calibration certificate.
  </p>
  <p>
    ISO 9001:2015 uses two different verbs on purpose. You <strong>maintain</strong> a document: you keep it current, and you
    expect it to change as your process changes. You <strong>retain</strong> a record: once it&rsquo;s created, it
    shouldn&rsquo;t change, because it&rsquo;s evidence of a point in time. Mixing the two up causes real problems. Editing a
    record after the fact is a finding waiting to happen. Never updating a document that everyone has quietly stopped
    following is another. ISO 9001:2026 keeps the same underlying idea but drops the two separate verbs. It uses one phrase
    throughout the standard instead: documented information being "available" as the document or the evidence needs.
    It&rsquo;s a wording change. You still keep documents current and records unaltered. The standard
    now describes both with one term.
  </p>

  <h2>What does clause 7.5 require?</h2>
  <p>
    Clause 7.5 has three parts, and each asks for something distinct. Read together, they describe a full lifecycle: decide what
    you need, create and approve it properly, then keep it under control for as long as it&rsquo;s in use. Clause 7.5 keeps its
    numbering and this same three-part shape in both ISO 9001:2015 and ISO 9001:2026, so everything below applies to whichever
    edition your organisation is certified to.
  </p>
  <h3>7.5.1: general</h3>
  <p>
    You must maintain the documented information the standard explicitly asks for, plus whatever documented information your own
    organisation decides it needs for the QMS to be effective. How much that is scales with your size, the complexity of your
    processes and the competence of your people. A five-person business and a 200-person one will land on different
    amounts of paperwork, and that&rsquo;s by design.
  </p>
  <h3>7.5.2: creating and updating</h3>
  <p>
    When you create or change a document, identify it properly with a title, a reference number, a date or revision, and an
    author, and put it in a suitable format, whether that&rsquo;s a PDF, a printed sheet on a wall, or a page in a system.
    Then review and approve it for suitability and adequacy before anyone relies on it. Skipping the approval step is one of the
    most common shortcuts small teams take, and one of the easiest for an auditor to catch.
  </p>
  <h3>7.5.3: control of documented information</h3>
  <p>
    Once a document exists, you have to keep controlling it. Make sure it&rsquo;s available and suitable for use where and when
    it&rsquo;s needed, and adequately protected from loss of confidentiality, improper use or loss of integrity. Clause 7.5.3.2
    lists the specific activities this covers: distribution, access, retrieval and use, and storage and preservation,
    including keeping it legible. It also covers control of changes, such as version control, and retention and disposition,
    meaning how long you keep it and what happens to it after that.
  </p>

  <h2>What&rsquo;s the difference between a controlled and an uncontrolled document?</h2>
  <p>
    A controlled document is managed through a defined process from creation to retirement: someone owns it, changes go through
    review and approval, and there is always one current version that people can point to. An uncontrolled document is a copy
    that has stepped outside that process (most often a printout, a saved PDF on someone&rsquo;s desktop, or an email
    attachment) and can go out of date without anyone knowing, because nothing links it back to the controlled original.
  </p>
  <p>
    You don&rsquo;t have to ban printing. Many organisations mark printed copies "uncontrolled when printed" so it&rsquo;s clear
    they may not reflect the current version. What matters to an auditor is whether people are working from something
    current.
  </p>

  <h2>What does the document lifecycle look like in practice?</h2>
  <p>
    Clause 7.5 doesn&rsquo;t hand you a named lifecycle, but almost every working document control system runs the
    same sequence of stages.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Create</strong>: someone drafts the document and gives it an identity: title, number, revision.</li>
    <li><strong>Review</strong>: a competent person checks it&rsquo;s technically correct and fit for its purpose.</li>
    <li><strong>Approve</strong>: an authorised person signs it off before it goes live.</li>
    <li><strong>Issue</strong>: the approved version is made available to the people who need it, and any old version is withdrawn from active use.</li>
    <li><strong>Revise</strong>: when something changes, the document goes back through review and approval as a new version, with the previous one kept on file.</li>
    <li><strong>Archive</strong>: once a document is no longer in use, it&rsquo;s retained for whatever period you&rsquo;ve set, then disposed of.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Most document control failures happen at the joins between these stages: a revision that skips review, or an old
    version that never gets withdrawn from a shared drive. A system that enforces the sequence, rather than relying on someone
    remembering it, removes most of that risk.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you control documents of external origin?</h2>
  <p>
    Clause 7.5.3.2 specifically extends control to documented information of external origin that your organisation determines
    is necessary for planning and operating the QMS. In practice, that&rsquo;s anything you rely on but didn&rsquo;t write
    yourself: the ISO 9001 standard, industry standards, customer drawings and specifications, regulatory requirements, and
    supplier certificates such as certificates of analysis, safety data sheets and statements.
  </p>
  <p>
    The control principle is the same as for your own documents: know which version you&rsquo;re relying on, and know when
    it changes. The mechanism is different, because you don&rsquo;t control the source. In practice that usually means
    a register of external documents, an owner for each one, and a way of noticing when a certificate is about to expire or a
    standard is revised. See our guide on <a href="/guides/document-control-procedure">building a document control procedure</a>
    for a worked example that covers external documents specifically.
  </p>

  <h2>Is a document control procedure mandatory in ISO 9001:2015?</h2>
  <p>
    No. Unlike the 2008 version of the standard, ISO 9001:2015 doesn&rsquo;t name "control of documents" as one of the mandatory
    documented procedures, and ISO 9001:2026 carries this forward unchanged. What&rsquo;s mandatory is the outcome in clause
    7.5.3. Most organisations write a short procedure anyway, because
    it&rsquo;s the easiest way to make sure everyone follows the same steps and to answer an auditor&rsquo;s questions
    consistently.
  </p>

  <h2>What do auditors typically check, and where do findings come from?</h2>
  <p>
    An auditor working through clause 7.5 usually samples a handful of documents and follows them end to end: is the version in
    use the approved one, is it legible and accessible to the people who need it, was it properly reviewed, and are old versions
    out of circulation. They&rsquo;ll also ask how you handle documents of external origin, and whether records are retained for
    as long as your own rules say they should be.
  </p>
  <p>
    The table below maps the core requirements to a practical control and the kind of evidence an auditor typically asks to see.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Requirement</th>
          <th>Practical control</th>
          <th>Evidence an auditor asks for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {requirementTable.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.clause}</td>
            <td>{r.control}</td>
            <td>{r.evidence}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The most common findings come from the gap between what a procedure says and what happens: a printed copy on a wall
    that&rsquo;s two revisions behind the system, a supplier certificate nobody noticed had expired, or a document that was
    changed without going back through approval. None of these need a bigger system to fix. They need the lifecycle above
    to run the same way every time, for every document.
  </p>

  <h2>Does this still apply now that ISO 9001:2026 is out?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes. ISO published the sixth edition, ISO 9001:2026, on 16 September 2026, and clause 7.5 keeps the same numbering and the
    same substance in the new edition, with the maintain/retain wording unified as described above. ISO 9001:2015 remains a
    valid, currently auditable standard through a three-year transition period, with 2015 certificates staying valid until
    around 30 September 2029, so nothing on this page stops applying because a new edition exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your organisation is already certified to ISO 9001:2015, you don&rsquo;t need to act immediately. Certification
    bodies will confirm their own transition timelines as they become accredited to audit the 2026 edition. If you&rsquo;re
    starting your first certification now, ask your certification body which edition they&rsquo;re auditing against, since both
    are current at the same time during the transition window.
  </p>

  <h2>How does Obel MS support document control today?</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/product/external-document-control">External Document Control</a> is built directly around clause 7.5.3.2: you
    upload a supplier certificate or standard, accept, edit or dismiss the AI-suggested metadata, then approve it, which moves it
    into the Register with status Validated. Every document is auto-filed into a Supplier &rarr; Manufacturer &rarr; Product
    folder, and its status (Active, Expiring soon, Expired, or No expiry set) is worked out automatically, so an
    expired certificate doesn&rsquo;t sit unnoticed until an audit finds it. Every upload, approval and new version is recorded
    in a full audit trail.
  </p>
